ArchiveEntry "R3-note: l1 sign and global loss-of-sync are independent (decoupling witness)"

/* R3-note: the first Lyapunov coefficient sign sign(l1) is NOT the correct criterion
 * for global loss of synchronization.  Reason (parametric): l1 governs only the LOCAL
 * Hopf normal form near the electrical onset Sigma_1 (an R1 object), whereas global
 * loss of sync is governed by the FOLD Sigma_2 (an R2 object, the unbalance boundary
 * |M|=Vg).  These two conditions are algebraically INDEPENDENT: one can hold while the
 * other fails.  dL-provable decoupling witness: there exist parameter values with
 * l1 < 0 (locally supercritical, 'safe' by the Bautin reading) AND simultaneously
 * past the fold (M^2 > Vg^2, no sync equilibrium => global loss of sync).  Their
 * conjunction is satisfiable, so l1<0 does NOT imply a forward-invariant neighborhood. */
Problem
  \exists l1 \exists M \exists Vg
  ( l1 < 0 & Vg > 0 & M^2 > Vg^2 )
End.
Tactic "decoupling witness by QE"
  QE
End.
End.

ArchiveEntry "R3-note: l1<0 does not entail existence of an invariant basin (no implication)"

/* The implication 'l1 < 0  ->  (sync basin nonempty)' is FALSE as a parametric law:
 * basin-nonemptiness requires a sync equilibrium, i.e. below the fold (M^2 <= Vg^2),
 * which is a condition on (M,Vg) entirely separate from l1.  Formalized: it is NOT
 * the case that l1<0 forces M^2 <= Vg^2.  (We prove the negation is satisfiable:
 * l1<0 together with M^2 > Vg^2.)  Hence sign(l1) cannot be the loss-of-sync test. */
Problem
  \forall l1 \forall M \forall Vg
  ( ( l1 < 0 & Vg > 0 & M^2 > Vg^2 )
    -> !(M^2 <= Vg^2) )
End.
Tactic "no implication by QE"
  QE
End.
End.
